    <HorizontalCard
    title="MSc Dissertation: ML–Interpolation for Pharmaceutical Manufacturing"
    desc="Predicting Mean Residence Time (MRT) in Twin-Screw Granulation when experimental data is scarce and expensive. I built a two-stage framework that used 24 physical observations as anchor data, held out test blocks before interpolation, and generated denser datasets using RBF, Matérn Gaussian Process Regression and penalised P-splines. I then evaluated NARX, Dual NARX, Ridge-ARX and RBF-SVR-ARX models, studied how interpolation density affects accuracy, and assessed results with RMSE, MAE, R² and error analysis. The project took me from literature and process understanding through to implementation, critical evaluation and scientific reporting."
    badge="THESIS"
    tags={["Python", "Gaussian Process Regression", "P-splines", "NARX", "SVR", "Ridge Regression", "Process Modelling"]}
  />
  <div class="divider my-0"></div>
  <HorizontalCard
    title="M5 Sales Forecasting: End-to-End ML Pipeline"
    desc="Predicting how much of each product will sell, in each store, next month: the line between empty shelves and dead stock. With my classmates, I built a scalable pipeline on the M5 Forecasting (Kaggle) dataset to forecast monthly item-store sales. We engineered features across hierarchical sales, calendar and pricing data, trained LightGBM and Gradient Boosted Trees, and focused evaluation on business-ready accuracy for inventory and marketing decisions, not just leaderboard scores."
    badge="TEAM PROJECT"
    tags={["Databricks", "PySpark", "LightGBM", "Gradient Boosted Trees", "Feature Engineering", "Time Series"]}
  />
  <div class="divider my-0"></div> 
  
  <div class="divider my-0"></div>
  <HorizontalCard
    title="Crop Yeild Prediction"
    img="/agri.jpg"
    desc="Its a web-based application designed to recommend suitable crops based on soil and weather conditions. The system aims to minimize human errors and provide accurate, real-time recommendations to assist farmers in selecting the best crops for their specific environment."
